This rule applies to Laravel web applications (`laravel-webapp`). Do not
apply it to Roots/Radicle/Sage WordPress projects (`roots-radicle`) or
other non-Laravel stacks.

### Backend

For tasks involving:

- Laravel
- Livewire
- Business logic
- Database design
- APIs
- Performance
- Architecture
- Refactoring
- Testing

Automatically consult Laravel Boost when available. Laravel web
applications are expected to have Boost; if it is missing, diagnose it
and continue with Current-First official docs. Do not add Boost to
user-global MCP. Install Boost only with Laravel's native installer
(`composer require laravel/boost --dev` then `php artisan boost:install`)
after approval.

# Laravel Rules

## Architecture

-   Follow Laravel conventions appropriate to the project's installed
    Laravel ecosystem (or the verified current stable ecosystem for
    greenfield). Do not use historically familiar conventions from model
    memory — see Current-First Engineering in `00-platform`.
-   Follow Framework-Native First in `00-platform`: use the installed
    Laravel ecosystem's native capabilities before parallel
    infrastructure or extra packages.
-   Keep controllers responsible only for HTTP request and response
    handling.
-   Place business logic in services or domain classes.
-   Keep models focused on persistence, relationships, casts, and
    scopes.
-   Prefer dependency injection over facades when practical.

## Database

-   Use Eloquent unless there is a demonstrated performance reason not
    to.
-   Use foreign key constraints.
-   Follow Laravel naming conventions.
-   Create focused migrations.

## Frontend

Preferred stack (unversioned; subordinate to Current-First Engineering
in `00-platform`):

-   Blade
-   Livewire
-   Alpine.js
-   Tailwind CSS

Avoid unnecessary JavaScript frameworks unless the project requires
them.

## Code Quality

-   Follow existing project conventions when they correctly use the
    installed stack. Do not copy scattered framework misuse.
-   Favor readability over cleverness.
-   Build the simplest solution that satisfies the requirements.

## Laravel Boost

Laravel web applications are expected to install Boost with Laravel's
native installer (`composer require laravel/boost --dev`,
`php artisan boost:install`). Boost owns project `.cursor/mcp.json`.
The AI Platform diagnoses the capability; it does not write Boost config.

When Laravel Boost is available:

- Use Laravel Boost as the primary source for framework guidance.
- Prefer Boost recommendations over generic Laravel knowledge.
- Follow established project patterns when they correctly use the
  installed stack (Framework-Native First in `00-platform`).

When Boost is missing, report the gap and use Current-First official
documentation. Do not invent a platform MCP entry for Boost.

## Browser QA

This profile is browser-capable. Capability is not authorization. Do not
invoke Playwright or browser MCP unless the current task explicitly
authorizes it (`60-browser-qa`).

When authorized, follow the project's `docs/contracts/features/browser-qa.md`
when present. Platform policy: two-layer Mode A (Playwright specs) + Mode B
(agentic browser MCP). A meaningful Mode B defect should normally become
Mode A coverage.

Laravel / Livewire:

- Account for Livewire synchronization; generic `fill()` often does not
  update `wire:model`.
- Choose one validation boundary. HTML5 validation can block Livewire-owned
  validation.
- Validation errors must behave correctly as users repair fields.
- Browser-history resubmission is a real duplicate-creation path on create
  workflows.
- Test status transitions and lifecycle workflows, not only CRUD.
- Name and clean up only test-owned records. Preserve seeded/demo data.
- Do not drain shared development queues; process only test-owned work.
- Dashboard/list assertions must identify test-owned records, not global
  counts.
- Prefer role/label selectors; sparse `data-testid`.
- Fake or stub external integrations in browser tests.

The AI Platform repository owns the reusable policy; this project's
`docs/contracts/features/browser-qa.md` owns commands and coverage.

# Laravel Web Application Profile

## Purpose

This profile applies to standard Laravel web applications built using
the preferred development stack. It does not apply to Roots/Radicle/Sage
WordPress projects (`roots-radicle`).

## Technology Stack

Preferred stack (unversioned; subordinate to Current-First Engineering
in `00-platform`):

-   Laravel
-   Livewire
-   Blade
-   Tailwind CSS
-   Alpine.js
-   MariaDB

## Architecture

-   Follow Laravel conventions for the installed or intentionally
    selected ecosystem (Current-First Engineering in `00-platform`).
-   Follow Framework-Native First in `00-platform`.
-   Keep controllers thin.
-   Place business logic in services or domain classes.
-   Keep models focused on persistence and relationships.
-   Prefer dependency injection.
-   Prefer existing patterns over introducing new ones when those
    patterns correctly use the installed stack.

## Development Principles

-   Keep solutions simple and maintainable.
-   Avoid unnecessary abstractions. Follow Framework-Native First
    before introducing wrappers or extra packages.
-   Make focused changes.
-   Do not perform unrelated refactoring.

## Documentation

Follow the `10-documentation` project rule.

Load relevant contracts from `docs/README.md` before backend work. Maintain
`docs/00-project-context.md`. Update affected contracts or report conflicts.
Re-export ChatGPT context when documentation, Cursor rules, agent skills, or
AI metadata change.
